From 497aa36adf94bf7c94196511338580bbf81b60d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:31:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(rust-rounds): the opening's takes carry over, newest
 kept per answer (epoch 25)

paulgsc/some-ui#3269, C736. Rust Rounds' essay opens on a standing
conversation of up to three questions, each answered by a take of its own,
`<week>:opening:<n>`. A week with no new take reuses the last one.

- A week may carry an `opening` (`/1` or `/2`): home reads its version and
  how many questions it asks, never its words.
- A voice note's `ref` may be `<week>:opening:<n>`, n 1 to 3
  (`voice_repo::is_rust_round_ref`), read as the week's, never a card's.
- A week's send may name the opening takes said that week, each
  `{n, take, recordedAt, version, ms?}`, under the week's version, one per
  question, each held as said for `<week>:opening:<n>`. They answer no
  ticket. A send without them reads and keeps as before.
- The week's record (and the rust index) says `opening`: for each
  question, the newest take a send of that week or an earlier one names
  under the week's version that was not deleted, with the week it was said
  in and its length when the phone measured it. Deleting one gives way to
  the next older; a later week's take never reaches an earlier week.
- An essay's opening names each take it plays. Home gives it only when each
  is the one the record resolves now, as `opening.<n>.<ext>` beside the
  tickets', and refuses it otherwise, so its words are always the take's.
  Deleting a take the given essay plays withdraws the week.

Contract epoch 25. No migration: sends are stored as JSON, and an old body
has no opening.

